### How to build your trees
- **No worktree is created in the NexusAI repo, and you never work in its `2_Project_Files` checkout or any builder `worktrees/` directory** (`worktrees/s89r-*`, `s87n-*`, `s86m-*`, incl. `s86m-rd816`). In that repo use ONLY read verbs: `show`, `diff`, `log`, `ls-tree`, `cat-file`, `grep`, `ls-remote`, `rev-parse`, `merge-base`, `rev-list` (plus `count-objects` for §7.9). **NEVER `fetch`, `pull`, `push`, `checkout`, `worktree`, `commit`, `stash`, `gc`, `merge`; and `merge-tree --write-tree` ONLY with `GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY=<your own scratch dir> GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES=<repo>/.git/objects`.**
- **Every tree is K1** — `git clone --shared --no-checkout <repo> <dir>` then `checkout` in YOUR clone (origin removed; local identity; `gc.auto 0`; `core.fsmonitor false`; hooks off), under a fresh `mktemp -d` in `/Volumes/KK_T9_External_HDD/!CODING/Testing Agent MAIN/projects/nexusai/qa-trees/batch18.XXXXXX/`. Status-check before use.
- **Each tree is EXCLUSIVE to this gate and to ONE purpose.** A fresh arm per mutant; never reuse a mutant tree for a clean arm; `batch18`-prefixed directories only. **Gates 14's, 15's and 17's trees are LIVE peers' — never run in them, never write to them, never clone node_modules FROM them** (nor from gates 12/13/16's).
- **The red-at-parent trees:** **A:** YOUR clone at `141d7ea` with ONLY `backend/azureLogAnalytics.js` (→ `595e71f`) and `backend/server.js` (→ `0d7e387`) restored, `lawEndpointPolicy.js` LEFT (the cells require it) — and a second arm with ALL THREE product files at `2f0ae4a` (the policy file removed in YOUR clone only; say which cells then fail to load) — the READY's "main 57/107 RED" was at `2f0ae4a` + test files; **B:** YOUR clone at `b79e02e` with ONLY `backend/dataExport.js` → `b31a4e0`; **C:** none — C changes no product file; its red is the mutant's (c0). Each hashed after the change, committed IN YOUR CLONE ONLY so each tree is a clean K1.
- **`node_modules` — TWO locks (WRONG 5, T2).** Each tree runs on node_modules PROVEN for its lock (`npm ls --all --offline` rc 0; every `invalid`/`extraneous`/`missing` line quoted; axios, http-cache-semantics, proxy-addr, sqlite3, playwright read back). **Source (H-31):** ONCE PER LOCK, `npm_config_update_notifier=false npm ci --offline --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund --cache "<your mktemp dir>/npm-cache"` in YOUR OWN K1 tree for that lock, the `_cacache` an APFS clone (`cp -c -R`) of `~/.npm/_cacache`; **then sqlite3's binding from an APFS clone of `~/.npm/_prebuilds` by sqlite3's own `prebuild-install` inside the strict belt, the log saying "found cached prebuild … unpacking", and a load proof** (ruling m). An `ENOTCACHED` is NOT RUN for that tree — name it and mail a QUESTION; **never go online.** Every other tree of the same lock gets node_modules by an APFS clone of YOUR proven tree. A real directory, never a symlink. **Never `npm install`.** Say that `--ignore-scripts` differs from CI's form (L-Y1).

## 2. Why these tiers, and who is waiting
- **RD-761 TIER 1 — severity rules:** **a token minted, or any request sent, for a refused endpoint (at a writer, at the sink, through the FALLBACK path, through a redirect, at boot from a stored value) is a Blocker (C-203 (a)); a request reaching any host other than the allowed constant — userinfo, port, scheme, case, trailing dot, IDN/punycode, `@`/backslash tricks, a 3xx followed — is a Blocker; a viewer's write accepted on a configured deployment (after first run, sign-in configured) is a Blocker (C-203 (b)); the open window changed (an anonymous allowed save refused, or a NEW surface closed) is a Major against C-178; a stored off-list value rewritten or migrated is a Major (C-203 (c)); a path, query, userinfo or credential in any log, audit, response or health body is a Major (Tuesday's 22:27Z ruling) — a host in an ANONYMOUS body is a Major; a non-GUID tenant reaching the token URL is a Major (C-203 (d)); the wizard showing "Configuration Saved Successfully" for a refused endpoint (a9) is a Major; a fixture-only file whose ASSERTIONS changed is a Major (C-97); a builder mutant not red where claimed is a Minor; a C-68 cell that passed at M0 failing on MT2/MTF is a Major; a READY claim that is false is a Minor (WRONG 1).**
- **RD-791 TIER 1 — severity rules:** **any byte of a linked store's TARGET, or the link TEXT, anywhere in the archive (manifest, entries, raw zip bytes) is a Blocker (C-139 ADDENDUM 1); a linked store marked `redacted: true`, or streamed through a redactor, is a Major; a dangling or looping store link silently absent from the manifest is a Major (F-A7 not closed); an ordinary store (file or declared directory) no longer streamed is a Major (C1's property); erasure changed is a Major (scope); the withheld wording naming a chain that ends at data "dangling" is Polish by default (T5); a builder mutant not red where claimed is a Minor; a C-68 cell that passed at M0 failing on MT1/MTF is a Major.**
- **RD-756 TIER 2 — severity rules:** **P9 not red under an INDEPENDENTLY re-derived M1 / M2 (L-B2 not closed) is a Major; P9 green for a reason other than the projection (e.g. the function is mocked, or the role never reaches it) is a Major; any product file in the delta is a Blocker for the tier claim (re-tier); a role shape P9 names but cannot see (a mutant that admits it with P9 green) is a Minor (C-40: a guard narrower than its claim); a C-68 cell that passed at M0 failing on MTF is a Major; a READY claim that is false is a Minor.**
- **Who is waiting:** RD-761's merge author is **NexusAI-R (S89R)** (now building RD-794); RD-791's is **NexusAI-N (S87N)** (merge turn now: RD-671); RD-756's (and RD-603's, and RD-816's) is **NexusAI-M (S86M)**. **Class cap:** RD-761 is round 1 of 2.

